[[File:Ampurias Tower from Tistaina Canal.png|thumb|The [[Tistaina Canal]], which bisects the primary business district of the [[Castejón Metropolis]].]]
The '''economy of the''' [[Castejón|'''Castejón Metropolis''']] encompasses the most powerful regional economy in the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] and is one of the most prominent in the world. In 2025, the metropolitan [[gross domestic product]] (PPP) amounted to US$1.482 trillion ({{Baliscan sol|1.976|link=yes}} trillion), making it the most powerful regional economy by gross GDP in [[W:Latin America|Latin America]], and the fourth most powerful globally, after [[W:Shanghai|Shanghai]], [[W:Tokyo|Tokyo]] and [[W:New York City|New York City]]. Anchored by the [[W:Alternative finance|alternative finance]] and high-tech sectors of the [[Ampurias]], [[Expansión]] and [[Trinacria]] boroughs, the city has been characterized as a global center of cooperative banking and innovation. A significant portion of the city's market activity is managed by the [[Cooperative Financial Exchange of Castejón|Cooperative Financial Exchange]], a confederal cooperative that facilitates ethical and transparent financial transactions among the network of [[W:Worker cooperative|cooperatives]], [[W:Cooperative federation|co-op federations]] and [[Essential industry|SOEs]].

The metropolis, with its core in the [[Alpujarra]], is the world's leading center of [[W:Economic democracy|cooperative finance]] and communication. Many of Balisca's most prominent enterprises, including the [[Luna Corporation]], [[Baliscana]], and the [[Baliscan Railways Group]], are based in the metropolis. The metropolitan area contains a vast number of worker cooperative offices and communal workspaces, but [[W:COVID-19|post-COVID-19]] [[W:Remote work|remote work]] policies have spurred a wave of [[Casajuan Plan|adaptive reuse]] projects, with many office spaces being repurposed into residential and communal spaces. The metropolis is a leading global center for the cooperative media, journalism, and publishing industries.

[[W:Cooperative finance|Cooperative finance]], high technology and [[W:Biotechnology|biotechnology]], the [[W:Right to the city|right to the city]], and [[Social protection in Balisca|social protection]] all form the basis of Castejón's economy. The city is also the confederation's most important center for mass media, journalism, and publishing, with the [[Castejón Broadcasting Collective]] and [[Castejón Communications (Castel)]] being two of the major players in the diverse cooperative media landscape. It is Balisca's preeminent arts and culture center, with a flourishing artist community. [[W:Creative industries|Creative industries]] such as digital media, advertising, fashion, design, and architecture account for a growing share of employment. The port facilities of [[Salto Bay]] are a major economic engine, though its focus has shifted from commercial shipping (with operations relocating to [[Jatiel]] in the 1990s due to environmental concerns) to a passenger-based transport and logistics system that operates at a scale far exceeding that of other cities in the Americas.

